# Microsoft vendor attributes for MS-CHAP and MS-CHAPv2.
#
# The `radius` package ships the RFC dictionaries but not Microsoft's, and these
# are the two attributes a MikroTik PPPoE server negotiates by default — so
# without this file a RADIUS server answers every login with Access-Reject and
# the reason is a silently unknown attribute rather than a wrong password.
#
# Deliberately typed `octets`, never `encrypt=1`. `encrypt=1` tells the package to
# run the RADIUS User-Password cipher (MD5 of the secret chained with the
# request authenticator) over the value, which is a completely different
# algorithm from what a CHAP response is. Applying it would "decrypt" the
# challenge into nonsense and make every CHAP login fail for a reason that looks
# like a bad password. A CHAP response is transmitted in the clear, like PAP, and
# the security comes from the challenge it answers — not from the transport.

VENDOR Microsoft 311

BEGIN-VENDOR Microsoft

# RFC 2439 — MS-CHAP
VENDORATTR 311 MS-CHAP-Challenge 11 octets
VENDORATTR 311 MS-CHAP-Response 1 octets

# RFC 2759 — MS-CHAPv2
VENDORATTR 311 MS-CHAP2-Challenge 25 octets
VENDORATTR 311 MS-CHAP2-Response 1 octets
VENDORATTR 311 MS-CHAP2-Verify 11 octets

# RFC 3079 / 2439 — the peer challenge MS-CHAP2 sends back in its Access-Challenge
VENDORATTR 311 MS-Peer-Challenge 16 octets

END-VENDOR Microsoft
